portfolio
Project Title
From WhatsApp Chaos to Kitchen Command Center: A Zero-Cost Order Management System for a Local Food Delivery Business

The Challenge
My client ran a thriving local food delivery operation — but success was breaking the system. Every order came through WhatsApp and was handled entirely by hand. The owner was simultaneously cooking and answering messages, which created a perfect storm: orders slipped through the cracks, the kitchen fell behind, and the operational load was crushing.
Two hard constraints made this a genuinely interesting problem to solve:

The end customers weren't tech-savvy. Many were older or unfamiliar with technology, so forcing them to download a new app or create an account was off the table. Whatever I built had to keep WhatsApp as the front door.
The budget for infrastructure was $0/month. No server bills, no SaaS subscriptions, no hosting fees. The solution had to be lean enough to run essentially for free, forever.

In short: deliver enterprise-grade order management on a bootstrap budget, without asking anyone — customer or owner — to change their habits.

The Solution & Architecture
Rather than reinventing the customer experience, I worked with existing behavior. WhatsApp stayed exactly as it was. Customers simply receive a single, friction-free form link — no app, no signup, no learning curve.
Here's where the creativity comes in. Instead of provisioning (and paying for) a traditional database and server stack, I turned the Google Sheets API into a real-time, zero-cost database. Every order submitted through the form flows automatically into a structured spreadsheet that doubles as the system's backbone — reliable, free, and instantly auditable by the owner if needed.
From there, the data powers two connected experiences:

A visual Kanban dashboard for the kitchen, built in React, organizing every order across four clear stages — Novo, Preparando, Pronto, Cancelado — with live counters so the owner can see the entire operation at a glance. Real-time updates keep the board perfectly in sync as orders move.
A Node.js backend wired directly to a local thermal printer (ESC/POS protocol). The moment an order lands, a physical ticket prints automatically in the kitchen — no copying, no manual entry, no missed orders.

The whole system launches with a single click from the desktop thanks to a remote installation setup, so the owner never has to touch a terminal or worry about technical maintenance.
The result is an architecture that's elegantly minimal: a familiar messaging channel on the front end, a free cloud spreadsheet as the data layer, and a lightweight React + Node.js stack tying it all together with real-time hardware integration.

Business Impact & Results

$0 in fixed monthly infrastructure costs — the system runs on free-tier tooling by design, so it scales with the business instead of eating into margins.
Zero lost or missed orders. Automatic ticket printing and a real-time board eliminated the manual handoffs where orders used to disappear.
Massive reduction in operational load. The owner went from juggling messages and cooking to running a calm, visual kitchen workflow — freeing up time and mental bandwidth to focus on the food.
No change required from customers. By keeping WhatsApp as the channel, adoption was instant and effortless for a non-technical audience.
One-click startup. The owner runs the entire system without any technical knowledge.

This project is a clear demonstration of solving a real business problem with smart, resourceful engineering — not by throwing money at infrastructure.

